OneiOS take24, stack 555b13d (NativeMetal 935c468), patches-555b13d/NativeMetal/0012. No switch (a key and one log line).
Asked: "every new build's first boot compiles all shaders again although the compilers did not change".
Read first, what a Data store that ran three CI builds of the same compilers showed: the shader cache is NOT keyed by the plug-in's
build. The translations live under <cache>/v1/<SHA-256 of the translator's version, artifact versions and source digest> (the
runtime's shader_cache, this_build()), the driver's pipeline data is named by Turnip's pipelineCacheUUID, which the base's
build-turnip-darwin.py derives from Mesa's pin, its patches' diff and its extra sources (-Dtu-build-id), not from the dylib's
LC_UUID or its file time. Three builds with three LC_UUIDs and CDHashes wrote into one translator directory under one
pipelineCacheUUID, and the newest's first boot wrote a twentieth of what the first boot after the stack's change had written.
The one key that did follow the build: the priming pass's (PrimeShaders.h buildId(): the runtime's id and Turnip's LC_UUID, which
every link changes), so each CI build primed its list's libraries again.
What: (1) buildId() ends in ONEIOS_TURNIP_BUILD, the id Turnip's pipeline cache is keyed by, from OneiOSTurnipBuild.h, which
userland/metal/build.sh writes beside this file from what build-turnip-darwin.py passed as -Dtu-build-id; the LC_UUID only
without that header. A Mesa pin, patch or extra source that changes still primes again, and so does a translator change
(nmb_runtime_build), as before. The first boot of this patch primes once more (the id's form changed: an old line cannot be
proved to be the same Turnip source, so none is adopted). (2) The render server says once a boot, 60 s after its first render
pipeline, and at the prewarm pass's end, what the kept cache did: `native-metal: shader cache (WHEN): F found, N not found,
W written; kept by translator T, Turnip U` (the device's counts, nmb_device_work_stats). Two builds that print the same T and U
read each other's files.
Risk: low. (1) changes only when a library is primed again; (2) reads counters. Removable: yes
(ONEIOS_METAL_SKIP=prime-key-and-cache-line-by-compiler-ids). The CI is its first compile; nothing of it has run.
Applies only after: 0009-render-pipelines-kept-and-made-at-boot.patch 0011-archive-asks-made-in-the-background.patch

